#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065335 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "40623a7d-e08a-5ec3-a3d6-1fac6307b20a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1275 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
